		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=4411&amp;p=32985#p32985"><![CDATA[
<blockquote class="uncited"><div>Noticed that Anytone has included satellite support in the 3.04 release for 878UV+ &amp; 878UVII+<br>I guess they got the idea from OpenGD77.<br><br>The radar position seems about the same but time and elevation is totally different on the 878<br><br>RT3-878.JPG</div></blockquote>Someone emailed me links to YT videos about this today.<br><br>Looks like Anytone copied all of the OpenGD77 satellite functionality<br><br>Prediction on the Anytone seems to be very very slow, I was also told that sometimes its incorrect.<br>They also can't spell "predicting", adding an extra "i" into their version of this word <img class="smilies" src="http://localhost/opengd77/images/smilies/icon_e_wink.gif" width="15" height="17" alt=";-)" title="Wink"><br><br>The OpenGD77 predictions have been verified by many people against the AMSAT web page predictions and also against various apps, and have always been correct.<br><br>Check the Anytone predictions against the AMSAT website etc<br><br>Also be aware that the predictions are only as good as the keps data.  Do you know where Anytone sources their keps ?<br>With OpenGD77 you can specify from where you download the keps.<br>The default is <a href="https://celestrak.org/NORAD/elements/amateur.txt" class="postlink">https://celestrak.org/NORAD/elements/amateur.txt</a>  because normally this is the most accurate source.<br><br>PS.<br><br>I'm not an expert in satellite operation, but this functionality is tested by people who are very expert in this.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=50">VK3KYY</a> — Tue Sep 17, 2024 7:26 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
